\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Classification of successive seed spacings. The dashed lines are the ISO-style boundaries at half and one-and-a-half times the target spacing. A double close to a nominal site and a skip at the next site are one multiple and one miss, not two noisy singles.}}{4}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Schematic row unit. The hopper and the plate sit on a timber link of length \(L\). Soil force at the boot deflects the link. The seed falls a height \(H\) after release, so any horizontal velocity of the moving gate becomes a spacing error.}}{6}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Latency budget of a corrective vision loop. Detection can be accurate and still harmful if the actuator command arrives after the empty cell has passed the release gate.}}{9}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces The two literatures meet the timber flexure at one variable. Vision metering can state a speed it is able to correct. Coverage can state a speed and a heading that save energy. Neither statement is safe unless both are true at once.}}{17}{figure.caption.10}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Compliance-aware vision metering. The vehicle is influenced only through the feasible-speed set. Plate speed and boot depth stay on the row unit.}}{25}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Plate observation. The detector labels cells before they reach the gate. One empty cell is still repairable. A seed already in the wooden tube is only a measurement of release time, not a seed that can be called back.}}{28}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Modelled quality-of-feed index against speed at zero acceleration, from Equations~\eqref {eq:openmiss} and \eqref {eq:closedmiss}. The closed-loop curve holds near \num {0.98} while repairs are timely and falls once Equation~\eqref {eq:cfrac} withdraws repair authority, then falls again through the timber resonance near \SI {2.4}{m/s}.}}{33}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces The same rectangle covered along the slope and along the contour. Long uphill rows pay a sustained slip loss. Short contour rows pay a turn, and an acceleration, at every swath.}}{40}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Inner horizon. Tracking error is penalised, and so is any predicted speed that approaches the timber resonance or any predicted acceleration that would make a repair late.}}{44}{figure.caption.17}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Four-row build. Metering tasks stay on the row computers' detector threads. The tracker sees a feasible speed set and a realised speed, not a private copy of the plate state.}}{56}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Two clocks. The row detector is fast enough to see every cell several times at the design speed. The tracker is an order of magnitude slower, which is the separation the joint problem was split along.}}{58}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Modelled depth root-mean-square error. The dashed line is Requirement~R2. The peak is the timber resonance, which the speed set is required to avoid.}}{62}{figure.caption.24}%
